          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A sacrococcygeal teratoma in a newborn most likely arises from persistent remnan</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A sacrococcygeal teratoma in a newborn most likely arises from persistent remnants of which embryonic structure?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Notochord</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Notochordal remnants can give rise to chordoma, a rare malignant tumour of the axial skeleton, not sacrococcygeal teratoma.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Primitive streak</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! The primitive streak normally regresses by the end of the fourth week. Persistent remnant cells near the tailbone can proliferate to form a sacrococcygeal teratoma, the most common solid tumour in newborns.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Neural crest cells</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Neural crest cells migrate throughout the embryo to form ganglia, Schwann cells, and melanocytes — they are not the source of sacrococcygeal teratomas.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Neural tube</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Failure of neural tube closure produces neural tube defects such as spina bifida and anencephaly, not teratomas.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the fate of the primitive streak: it regresses by the end of the fourth week. Persistent remnant cells near the tailbone can form a sacrococcygeal teratoma — the most common solid tumour in newborns."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Sacrococcygeal teratoma arises from primitive streak cells that fail to regress by the fourth week. It is mostly benign at birth but becomes malignant if not removed promptly."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
