          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Neural tube closure proceeds bidirectionally from the cervical region. Failure o</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Neural tube closure proceeds bidirectionally from the cervical region. Failure of the caudal neuropore to close on day 27 results in which condition?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Anencephaly</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Anencephaly results from failure of the cranial neuropore to close on day 25, not the caudal neuropore. It is a condition incompatible with life.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Chordoma</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Chordoma arises from notochordal remnants within the vertebral column, not from failure of neural tube closure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Spina bifida</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! Failure of the caudal neuropore to close on day 27 produces spina bifida, ranging from asymptomatic spina bifida occulta to severe myelomeningocele with paralysis and bladder dysfunction.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Sacrococcygeal teratoma</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Sacrococcygeal teratoma arises from persistent primitive streak cells that fail to regress by the fourth week, not from neural tube closure failure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember the directional rule: cranial neuropore (day 25) failure causes anencephaly; caudal neuropore (day 27) failure causes spina bifida."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Caudal neuropore closure failure produces spina bifida. Periconceptional folic acid at 0.4–5 mg/day reduces the risk of neural tube defects by 70%."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
